equences. Your other chow is character W7,W8,W9, which fits the 7-8-9 sequence. So you need two more chows: one in bamboo that is either 1-2-3 or 4-5-6, and one in dots that is the remaining sequence. But your bamboo chow is 2-3-4, so it doesn't help. You could try to change your hand, but it's late game and you have exposed melds, so they are fixed. Therefore, Mixed Straight is not possible with your current melds. Alternatively, you could aim for All Chows (four chows and a pair) which is worth 2 points, but you need additional patterns to reach 8. All Chows plus All Simples (2 points) plus maybe Self-Drawn (1 point) etc., still might not reach 8. Looking at your hand, you have many simple tiles, so All Simples (2 points) is possible. You also have two chows already, so if you form two more chows and a pair, you get All Chows (2 points). That totals 4 points, still need 4 more. You could get points from Pung of Terminals or Honors (but you have none), or from Closed Wait, Edge Wait, etc. It's challenging. Perhaps you should consider discarding the lone B6 because it's isolated and doesn't pair well. Keeping the pairs (T2 and B2) gives you better chances to complete pungs if you draw the third tile, and pungs can give points (e.g., All Pungs worth 6 points, or Pung of Simples worth maybe 1 point if it's a pung of simples? Actually, Pung of Terminals or Honors is worth 1, but pung of simples gives no points by itself). So if you form a pung of W5, that's not worth points by itself unless it's part of All Pungs. If you get three pungs and a pair, All Pungs gives 6 points, plus maybe All Simples 2 points, that's 8. So aiming for All Pungs might be a good strategy. You have two chows exposed, so you cannot have All Pungs because All Pungs requires four pungs/kongs. You already have two chows, so at most you can have two pungs. So All Pungs is impossible. Therefore, your hand is likely to be a low-scoring hand, and you need to maximize the chance to win by drawing a winning tile soon. Considering tile efficiency: discarding B6 keeps your hand with two pairs and a triple, which is relatively close to needing either one more pair (for Seven Pairs) or one more meld. But Seven Pairs requires seven distinct pairs, and you have only two pairs and a triple (which could become a pair if you discard one W5, but then you have three pairs). Actually, if you discard B6, you have: W5,W5,W5 (three tiles), T2,T2, B2,B2. That's two pairs and a set of three identical tiles. To win with a standard hand, you need one more meld and a pair. The pair can be either T2 or B2, so you need a meld from the remaining tiles. The meld could be a pung of W5 (you already have three, so it's a concealed pung), so you actually have a concealed pung of W5 already. Then you have two chows (exposed), one concealed pung, and you need one more meld and a pair. You have two pairs: T2 and B2. So if you use one pair as the final pair, you need one more meld from the other pair (by drawing the third tile to make it a pung) or form a chow with the single B6? But B6 is discarded. So after discarding B6, your hand is: concealed pung W5, pair T2, pair B2. You need to draw either a third T2 or a third B2 to make a pung, and then you have four melds (two chows, two pungs) and a pair. That would be a winning hand. So the winning tiles are: T2 (only 2 left in unshown? Actually, unshown T2:2, but you hold 2 T2, so there are 0 left? Wait, unshown tiles list: T2:2. That means there are 2 T2 tiles not shown (not in your hand, not in melds, not discarded). You hold 2 T2, so total T2 in game: 4. Unshown:2 means there are 2 T2 tiles still in the wall or not revealed. So there are 2 T2 available. Similarly, B2: unshown 3, you hold 2, so 1 B2 available? Actually, unshown B2:3, you hold 2, so there is 1 B2 left? But unshown count includes tiles not seen by anyone, so yes, there is 1 B2 left. So you can win by drawing T2 (2 outs) or B2 (1 out). That's 3 outs. If you discard T2 instead, you have: W5 pung, B2 pair, B6 single. Winning tiles: draw B2 to make B2 pung (1 out) and pair B6? Actually, then you would have: two chows, W5 pung, B2 pung, and you need a pair. You would need to pair B6, so you need to draw another B6 (unshown B6:2, you hold 1, so 1 left? Actually, unshown B6:2, you hold 1, so there is 1 B6 left? Wait, unshown B6:2 means there are 2 B6 not shown. You hold 1 B6, so there is 1 B6 left? Actually, total B6: 4. You hold 1, so 3 are elsewhere. Unshown:2 means 2 are in the wall not seen. So there are 2 B6 available. So winning tiles: draw B2 (1 out) and then draw B6 (2 outs) for pair, but that requires two draws, not immediate. So discarding T2 reduces your immediate winning chances. Discarding B2 similarly: W5 pung, T2 pair, B6 single. Winning tiles: draw T2 (2 outs) to make T2 pung, and draw B6 (2 outs) for pair. Again, two draws needed. Discarding W5: you would have W5 pair, T2 pair, B2 pair, B6 single. Then you need either to draw a third W5, T2, or B2 to make a pung and use the other two pairs as pairs, or go for Seven Pairs by drawing three more pairs. That's less efficient. So discarding B6 seems to give the best tile efficiency: you have two pairs and a pung, needing only one more pung from either pair, and then you have four melds and a pair. Moreover, you have two outs for T2 and one out for B2, total 3 immediate winning tiles if you draw them. Also, if you draw the fourth W5, you could declare a kong and then draw a replacement tile, which might also win. So keeping the W5 triple is good because it's already a meld, and it might become a kong for extra points. Therefore, I think discarding B6 is the best move.\\n\\nReason: Discarding B6 maintains the highest tile efficiency and number of winning tiles (3 outs: two T2 and one B2) to complete a standard hand with two chows, one concealed pung (W5), one more pung (from T2 or B2), and a pair (the other pair). This maximizes the chance to win quickly in the late game.\\n\\nAnswer: Play